Mr Wonka explains to Charlie how he went about inventing Vita-Wonk. His logic is wonderfully Dahl-ish: if you want to make something that makes people old, you must collect bits of the **oldest things in the world** and put them together.

So he sets off on a fantastic journey around the globe to collect samples. In his telling he gathers things like:

- pieces of the oldest **trees** on Earth (bristlecone-type pines thousands of years old);
- hair or bits from the oldest **animals** and creatures he could find;
- 'old' things from history and nature — ancient objects, very old stones, the oldest living things in remote places.

The list is deliberately absurd and exaggerated, full of made-up details. Dahl is having fun with science: real inventors test ideas carefully; Wonka's 'method' is fantasy logic.
